{"id":"supervision","ord":"a0","title":"The orchestrator","desc":"Read this subtree at the start of a conversation. Follow the human's instructions and use olai MCP to read and update the board. Keep lasting facts and decisions here so the next conversation can continue.\n\nWrite in simple, direct language. Use the agent roster and workflow below.\n\nRely on the Olai Kolu plugin's chat events to resume supervision. Do not start a separate Kolu watch or poll waiting terminals. The conversation's event scope must be orchestrator/lanes.olai. Keep terminal claims and task states current so the plugin can deliver events. See https://github.com/juspay/olai/blob/master/packages/plugins/kolu/docs.md.\n\nBrainstorming and investigation lanes remain open until the human takes action and directs their completion or cancellation. An agent finishing its report does not complete the lane. Mark it todo while awaiting the human; preserve the findings and lane tree.","created":"2026-08-25T16:28:07-04:00","changed":"2026-09-09T09:09:45-04:00","custom":{"chat-agent-session":"claude:28d8d259-a161-4024-accf-90eae22921ae"}}
{"id":"agents-root","parent":"supervision","ord":"aI","title":"Agent roster","desc":"The human chooses the implementor and one or two reviewers from this roster.","changed":"2026-09-08T10:27:38-04:00"}
{"id":"agent-claude-opus","parent":"agents-root","ord":"a0","title":"Claude Opus","desc":"Opus with a one-million-token context window. Check the model when it starts. Suggested text in Claude's message box is not an unsent human message.","changed":"2026-09-08T10:17:54-04:00","custom":{"command":"claude --model opus[1m] --dangerously-skip-permissions","role":"author"}}
{"id":"agent-claude-fable","parent":"agents-root","ord":"a3","title":"Claude Fable","desc":"Reviewer only; do not assign implementation work. Use when the human asks for Fable and check the model when it starts. Launch with the command below exactly: a plain `claude` opens Opus 5, which happened on 2026-09-11 (lane chat-sidebar-ux) and had to be relaunched. Fable may also design when the human asks for it.\n\nUsage limit: all Fable terminals share one account limit, shown in the terminal footer (\"You've used N% of your Fable limit · resets <date>\"). Read it when a lane starts and before a review; at 93% on 2026-09-12 00:30 UTC (reset Sep 13 10pm America/New_York) with two reviews still ahead, tell the human early so they can move a review to Opus or wait for the reset.","created":"2026-08-19T20:04:37-04:00","changed":"2026-09-11T19:57:35-04:00","custom":{"command":"claude --model fable --dangerously-skip-permissions","role":"reviewer"}}
{"id":"agent-opencode","parent":"agents-root","ord":"a5","title":"Opencode","desc":"Kimi author and reviewer. Messages sent while it works wait until later and do not interrupt it. If it fails or emits malformed tool-call text, type continue in its prompt. Do not kill or restart Opencode to recover from those failures; human explicitly corrected this on 2026-09-08.","changed":"2026-09-08T16:22:48-04:00","custom":{"command":"opencode","role":"author+reviewer"}}
{"id":"agent-codex","parent":"agents-root","ord":"a9","title":"Codex with GPT-6 Astra","desc":"GPT-6 Astra at medium reasoning effort. Runs this orchestrator and can also author or review when selected. Launch with --yolo as requested by the human.","created":"2026-09-08T10:05:43-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:48:44-04:00","custom":{"command":"codex --yolo --model gpt-6-astra -c 'model_reasoning_effort=\"medium\"'","role":"author+reviewer"}}
{"id":"workflow","parent":"supervision","ord":"aJ","title":"Workflow","desc":"This is the workflow template. When a workflow starts, copy this subtree into orchestrator/lanes.olai for that task, with new node IDs and wait dependencies pointing to the copied steps. Track progress and time on the copy; keep this template ready for the next task. Mark each copied step doing when it starts and done when it finishes. Keep review and CI steps open through their loops.","created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:30:59-04:00"}
{"id":"pes7ok2u","parent":"workflow","ord":"a0","title":"1. Human chooses the agents","todo":true,"desc":"The human picks one implementor and one or two reviewers.","created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:30:59-04:00"}
{"id":"1iitxclm","parent":"workflow","ord":"a1","title":"2. Start all agents","todo":true,"desc":"The orchestrator starts all two or three agents in Kolu terminals. Whenever a Kolu terminal is launched, immediately add its ID to the lane node's kolu-terminal property. Keep all terminal IDs there when a lane has multiple agents.","after":["pes7ok2u"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:34:23-04:00"}
{"id":"lrz5werw","parent":"workflow","ord":"a2","title":"3. Implement and open a draft PR","todo":true,"desc":"Send the human's prompt to the implementor. The implementor completes the work, opens a draft PR, and hands its URL back to the orchestrator. Record the URL in the lane node's pr-url property. Keep this step doing until the draft PR exists and its URL is recorded; only then mark it done and start the next steps.","after":["1iitxclm"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:37:15-04:00"}
{"id":"5btc98w7","parent":"workflow","ord":"a3","title":"4. Review until consensus","todo":true,"desc":"Send the completed PR to the chosen reviewer or reviewers. Each reviewer posts findings in a PR comment. Send the comments back to the implementor, then send the updated PR back to the reviewers. Repeat until the implementor and all chosen reviewers agree that the findings are resolved.","after":["lrz5werw"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:30:59-04:00"}
{"id":"hsffve7a","parent":"workflow","ord":"a4","title":"5. Refactor the PR","todo":true,"desc":"The implementor refactors the PR using https://kolu.dev/blog/hickey-lowy/: separate concepts tangled together (Hickey), and put boundaries around decisions that change independently (Löwy). Run relevant checks and hand the PR back to the orchestrator.","after":["5btc98w7"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T10:30:59-04:00"}
{"id":"x7urraby","parent":"workflow","ord":"a5","title":"7. Run Odu CI until it passes","todo":true,"desc":"Before starting CI, set the lane node's odu-worktree property to the checkout's absolute path. Run Odu CI through the Olai Odu plugin/MCP, using that same checkout. Rely on the plugin's chat events for failures and completion. Send failures to the implementor, who fixes them and hands the updated PR back for another run. Repeat until CI passes on the latest commit.","after":["workflow-merge-master"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T12:56:58-04:00"}
{"id":"2rq29dyr","parent":"workflow","ord":"a6","title":"9. Merge the PR","todo":true,"desc":"By default, report readiness and wait for the human to merge. If the human explicitly chooses auto-merge, merge only after CI passes on the latest PR commit and the required Saatchi evidence is published and verified.","after":["workflow-saatchi"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T12:56:58-04:00"}
{"id":"i67b5ppj","parent":"workflow","ord":"a7","title":"10. Close terminals and record time","todo":true,"desc":"After confirming the human's merge, close all terminals for the task. Record time spent on each step and the total on the work item, using the times recorded during the work.","after":["2rq29dyr"],"created":"2026-09-08T10:28:02-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T12:56:58-04:00"}
{"id":"workflow-saatchi","parent":"workflow","ord":"a5V","title":"8. Verify with Saatchi before auto-merge","todo":true,"desc":"Required when the human chooses auto-merge. The implementor uses https://github.com/juspay/saatchi to capture the actual app demonstrating the requested result on the latest PR commit and publishes the evidence on the PR. The reviewer inspects the evidence and records a verification verdict with the evidence link and reviewed commit. Keep auto-merge blocked until the reviewer verifies it. If verification requires code changes, send them to the implementor and repeat CI, evidence capture and reviewer verification on the updated commit. When the human will merge manually, mark this conditional step skipped. If an existing lane switches to auto-merge, add this step before its merge step. If no reviewer was selected, obtain the human's reviewer choice before this verification.","after":["x7urraby"],"created":"2026-09-08T12:50:36-04:00","changed":"2026-09-08T12:56:58-04:00"}
{"id":"workflow-merge-master","parent":"workflow","ord":"a4V","title":"6. Merge latest master","todo":true,"desc":"Before handing the PR to the orchestrator for CI, the implementor fetches and merges the latest origin/master into the PR branch, resolves any conflicts, runs relevant checks and pushes. If already up to date, record that verification. Hand back the resulting PR commit for CI. Repeat this check before each later CI handoff.","after":["hsffve7a"],"created":"2026-09-08T12:56:52-04:00"}
{"id":"dispatch-check","parent":"supervision","ord":"aK","title":"Confirm a handoff was submitted","desc":"After sending terminal text, waiting for output to settle and pressing Enter, inspect the terminal once to confirm the prompt was submitted or the agent started working. A successful sendInput response alone is not proof: Codex can leave pasted text in its input box. If still unsubmitted, press Enter again and verify. Do not report implementation underway until confirmed.\n\nCodex specifics (v0.153, observed 2026-09-09). A long paste shows as one or more \"[Pasted Content 1024 chars]\" placeholders. When Codex is IDLE, the first Enter expands the placeholders into the full text and a second Enter submits; a three-chunk paste has needed three Enters. Confirm with a screen read showing \"Working\" and an empty \"Ask Codex to do anything\" composer. When Codex is WORKING (mid-turn), the composer footer says \"tab to queue message\": Enter does not submit; press Tab to queue the message for its next turn, then confirm the composer is empty. Claude Code (Fable): a paste shows \"[Pasted text #N]\" and one Enter submits; a suggested line already in its composer is Claude's own suggestion, never an unsent human message.","created":"2026-09-08T15:28:29-04:00","changed":"2026-09-09T17:27:14-04:00"}
{"id":"ci-required-contexts","parent":"supervision","ord":"aL","title":"Check required CI before calling a PR green","desc":"Read target-branch rules with GitHub rules/branches endpoint and compare EVERY required context with successful checks/statuses on the exact current PR head. gh pr checks and statusCheckRollup omit expected contexts that never reported. Run every required platform; never call Linux-only Odu CI full when macOS is required. Odu master requires Linux and8 macOS contexts; Olai master currently requires Linux andCodeQL. Re-read rules per readiness check; do not assume forever.","created":"2026-09-08T18:32:42-04:00"}
{"id":"odu-incomplete-run","parent":"supervision","ord":"aM","title":"Read the coordinator log when Odu ends without deciding","desc":"An Odu event saying CI \"ended without deciding\" with N/M ok and nothing red is usually not a code failure. Read ~/.local/state/odu/runs/<runId>/coordinator.log first: on 2026-09-09 the aarch64-darwin venue ci@petit stopped answering keep-alive pings, the coordinator lost the lease and exited, which stopped every still-running node on both lanes (run 0mtucfuef-h06288us, 65/79 ok). Fix is a fresh run_start with a NEW requestId on the same head, not a message to the implementor. Do not pass linger: odu refuses --linger with sharded recipes, and a refused requestId stays consumed. If the macOS venue keeps dropping, a Linux-only run (platforms x86_64-linux) is acceptable for Olai, whose master requires Linux and CodeQL contexts only; Odu's master requires macOS too, so never do that for Odu.","created":"2026-09-09T13:05:04-04:00"}
{"id":"ci-host-health","parent":"supervision","ord":"aN","title":"Check CI host disk and load when unrelated tests time out","desc":"When an Odu run goes red on timeouts in tests the PR never touched, and different tests fail on each rerun, SSH the Linux venues before blaming the code or the implementor: `df -h /`, `/proc/loadavg`, `/proc/pressure/io`, and `ls /tmp/odu/olai | wc -l` on each host in ~/.config/odu/hosts.json. Do not clean or reboot the CI hosts yourself; report them to the human.\n\nFleet log. 2026-09-09 17:22 UTC: kolu-ci-5 and kolu-ci-9 at 100% disk, load near 30, then unreachable; every failing shard had landed on kolu-ci-5. 20:30 UTC: kolu-ci-8 and kolu-ci-10 at load 40–50 on 32 cores with almost no test processes; kolu-ci-8 shows IO pressure some avg60=72% with `bun install` and journald in D state, so its disk is I/O-saturated rather than full (340 G free); kolu-ci-7 intermittently unreachable over SSH. Healthy at that time: kolu-ci-2, kolu-ci-3, kolu-ci-6. Timeouts seen on the sick hosts: server boot \"never said where it was serving\", odu mcp probe, media 404 test, chat clear hook, olai MCP refusal tests, all 5 s limits.\n\nWorkarounds. For orchestrator full CI: run_start with hostsFile ~/.config/odu/hosts-lane-graph.json (lists kolu-ci-2, 3, 6, 7, 8, 10; trim further if more hosts sicken) and supersede a doomed run. For an implementor's own `just *-fast-remote` runs: odu reads `$ODU_HOSTS` first, so tell the agent to export ODU_HOSTS=/home/srid/.config/odu/hosts-lane-graph.json. The default ~/.config/odu/hosts.json is a read-only home-manager symlink into the nix store; only the human can change it. Two lanes running remote legs at once on a shrunken fleet makes the timeouts worse; if it persists, serialize the lanes' remote runs.","created":"2026-09-09T13:25:15-04:00","changed":"2026-09-09T16:28:50-04:00"}
{"id":"one-pr-per-lane","parent":"supervision","ord":"aO","title":"One PR per lane; never split into stacked PRs","desc":"Human's ruling 2026-09-09 23:05 UTC, restated angrily at 23:25 UTC, after the orchestrator split #545's implementation into three stacked PRs when the implementor stopped at a \"reviewable cut\" and the designer's document said \"each PR stays green on its own\": a lane delivers ONE PR. A design's numbered plan is an ordering of COMMITS inside that one PR, never a series of PRs. If the implementor says the diff is large, it still goes in one PR with one green commit per plan step; the reviewer reviews the whole PR once. Do not let an implementor stop at a split point, do not open a second PR for the same lane without the human asking for it, and ignore any designer or reviewer advice that implies multi-PR sequencing. \"Severable\" in a design means a later lane, not a parallel PR in this one. Fable was told this directly on 2026-09-09.","created":"2026-09-09T19:02:51-04:00","changed":"2026-09-09T19:16:25-04:00"}
{"id":"implementor-never-merges","parent":"supervision","ord":"aP","title":"Implementors never merge; say so in every implementation prompt","desc":"Incident 2026-09-10/11: the human asked the orchestrator to pass Fable's SEARCH-UX-PLAN.md to Codex \"as is, don't bias the prompt\". The plan said each item is a separate PR \"landed in the order below\" and \"run just ci after pushing\". Codex read \"landed\" as merge-it-yourself and squash-merged three PRs (#572 befa27caf, #573 5737292a6, #574 31860ad5e) into olai master with green full CI but no Fable review and no human merge decision, and was about to merge the fourth (#575) when the orchestrator interrupted it with Escape. Resolution ordered by the human 01:25 UTC 2026-09-11: origin/master force-pushed back to 7fa78120c (history deleted); Fable rewrote the plan as one PR; Codex rebuilt the four items as four squashed commits on one branch (search-ux) from its surviving branches and refs, resuming rather than starting afresh; the single PR then runs the normal review, refactor, CI and human-merge workflow.\n\nRules: (1) every prompt to an implementor, however brief, carries one sentence that it must not merge any PR and that the human merges after review; \"pass it as is\" applies to the design's content, not to this guard. (2) When a design document says \"land\", \"landed\" or \"merge\", the prompt says explicitly that landing means opening the PR. (3) A design that sequences several PRs is rewritten to one PR before implementation (see \"One PR per lane\"). (4) Codex's TUI: Escape interrupts a running turn; a queued message (Tab) only lands after the turn, too late to stop an action in flight; when Codex is idle, a paste can auto-submit as a new turn. (5) GitHub's delete-branch-on-merge removes merged branches, but merged PR heads stay reachable at refs/pull/N/head and the merge commits stay in any clone that fetched them, so a force-reset loses no diff.","created":"2026-09-10T20:04:30-04:00","changed":"2026-09-10T21:33:40-04:00"}
{"id":"implementor-stop-rule","parent":"supervision","ord":"aQ","title":"Implementors resolve mechanical plan mismatches themselves; stop only for design questions","desc":"Lesson from 2026-09-12 (lanes chat-sidebar-ux and file-kinds): the brief \"if the plan conflicts with the code in a way the plan does not resolve, stop and wait for Fable\" made Codex (GPT-6 Astra, medium) stop 22 and 16 times respectively, on everything from a missing slot to a one-line type addition and a menu label, each costing a Fable ruling and an orchestrator round trip, and once an eight-hour idle gap when the orchestrator missed the wake. The human asked why.\n\nRule for every implementation brief from now on: the implementor resolves MECHANICAL mismatches itself (a named file, signature, slot, contract field or scenario wording that differs from the tree; sequencing that needs a small reorder; a type the plan forgot) and records every such deviation in the PR body under \"Deviations from the plan\", one line each, for the reviewer to accept or reject. It STOPS only for DESIGN questions: who owns a thing, a new or changed contract or service that other rows see, user-visible behaviour the plan did not decide, or anything that would need a second PR. The reviewer reads the deviations list against the plan. This keeps the never-merge and one-PR rules unchanged.","created":"2026-09-12T08:00:55-04:00"}
